     "title": "MAGPIE rover heads for lunar polar ice exploration",
     "url": "https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/magpie-rover-heads-for-lunar-polar-ice-exploration-2026-08/",
     "time": "08-06 18:29",
     "ts": 1786012156,
     "summary": "ispace-EUROPE has won a €65 million ESA contract to develop Europe&#8217;s first lunar polar ice exploration rover, MAGPIE, the Mission for Advanced Geophysics ",
     "source": "Electronics Weekly",
     "content": "ispace-EUROPE has won a €65 million ESA contract to develop Europe’s first lunar polar ice exploration rover, MAGPIE, the Mission for Advanced Geophysics and Polar Ice Exploration.\nIt’s a Phase 2 award and funds the remaining rover and payload development, manufacture, testing, transportation, surface operations. And also the delivery of the science data to the European SPace Agency (ESA).\nThe rover will help will investigate water ice and regolith properties at the lunar south pole. This is a key enabling technology for any sustained presence on the Moon.\nMAGPIE\n“MAGPIE represents a major step forward for European lunar exploration,” said Julien-Alexandre Lamamy, CEO of ispace-EUROPE. “This contract award demonstrates ESA’s commitment to a new model for small, agile exploration missions — one that combines commercial delivery, scientific ambition and international collaboration.”\n“ispace-EUROPE is proud to lead the full delivery of MAGPIE, from mission design and rover development through to lunar surface operations and delivery of science data to ESA. This is exactly the kind of mission that can help Europe build operational capability on the Moon.”\nSophia Casanova, Senior Lunar Exploration Scientist at ispace-EUROPE, will serve as Principal Investigator for MAGPIE.\nJapan\nMAGPIE is due to launch with ispace Mission 4 in 2029. Lunar transportation costs will receive funding from Japan through an ESA–JAXA collaboration.\n“MAGPIE exemplifies ESA’s commitment to delivering ambitious exploration objectives through innovative, rapid-development missions and strong international cooperation,” added Sara Pastor, ESA’s Lunar Programme Manager.\n“By exploring and characterising volatile deposits in the lunar polar regions and searching for water ice that could support a sustained human presence on the Moon, MAGPIE will provide critical knowledge for the next phase of lunar exploration.”\nThe contract award follows ispace-EUROPE carrying out MAGPIE’s Design Prototype Review. Phase 2, which commenced on 1 July 2026, marks the beginning of full mission development,\nJaxa\nThe mission also reflects a growing cooperation between ESA and JAXA in lunar exploration, highlighted Pastor.\n“Bringing together leading industrial and scientific organisations from across Europe, ispace with MAGPIE demonstrates how ESA’s small missions for exploration can accelerate the development of high-value scientific and technological capabilities while strengthening Europe’s contribution to a sustainable future on the Moon.”\nispace-Europe is a subsidiary of its parent company, ispace, which is based in Japan.\nYou can read more on the ispace website.\nImage: ispace – (top) ipsace-EUROPE team (bottom) An artist’s impression of the MAGPIE rover design on the lunar surface\nSee also: ispace goes public to boost lunar transportation services",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "MAGPIE月球车将开展月球极地冰探测",
     "content_zh": "ispace-EUROPE公司获得欧空局6500万欧元合同，将开发欧洲首个月球极地冰探测月球车MAGPIE，该合同为第二阶段资助，覆盖月球车及载荷的开发、制造、测试、运输、表面作业，以及科学数据向欧空局的交付。该月球车将探测月球南极的水冰与表土性质，为月球长期驻留提供关键技术支撑。ispace-EUROPE CEO表示，MAGPIE是欧洲月球探测的重要里程碑，该合同体现了欧空局对小型敏捷探测任务新模式的投入，公司会负责从任务设计到月球表面作业、科学数据交付的全流程工作。"
