     "summary": "Gentari Australia has reached mechanical completion at the 243MWp Maryvale Solar and Energy Storage Project near Dubbo in New South Wales.",
     "source": "PV Tech",
     "content": "Gentari Australia, the local subsidiary of Malaysian clean energy company Gentari Renewables, has reached mechanical completion at the 243MWp Maryvale Solar and Energy Storage Project near Dubbo in New South Wales.\nThe milestone marks the end of physical construction on the solar-plus-storage site, with commissioning and grid connection works now expected to follow ahead of commercial operations.\nTry Premium for just $1\n- Full premium access for the first month at only $1\n- Converts to an annual rate after 30 days unless cancelled\n- Cancel anytime during the trial period\nPremium Benefits\n- Expert industry analysis and interviews\n- Digital access to PV Tech Power journal\n- Exclusive event discounts\nOr get the full Premium subscription right away\nOr continue reading this article for free\nThe project integrates 243MWp of solar generation capacity with a 172MW/409MWh lithium iron phosphate (LFP) battery energy storage system (BESS) in a DC-coupled configuration, meaning the solar array connects directly to the battery at the DC level before conversion to AC for grid export.\nThis configuration offers several advantages over traditional AC-coupled systems, including improved efficiency, reduced equipment costs and enhanced flexibility in energy dispatch.\nThe facility’s maximum export capacity into the National Electricity Market (NEM) is 172MW. It can import up to 91MW.\nPCL Construction served as the engineering, procurement and construction contractor. Gentari acknowledged PCL alongside a number of other partners in its announcement, including SOLMECH, Nextpower, Stralis Energy, All Energy Contracting, EPEC Group and Essential Energy.\nTrinaSolar supplied the solar PV modules and CATL supplied the battery storage system. Spanish inverter manufacturer Ingeteam was selected as the project’s inverter supplier in a deal that marked the first DC-coupled solar-plus-storage project in eastern Australia to proceed to construction.\nThe tracker system was supplied by Canadian racking company Polar Racking, which secured the Maryvale contract as its first Australian project, in a deal that gave the company a foothold in the country’s rapidly expanding utility-scale solar market.\nThe project is located within the Central-West Orana Renewable Energy Zone, approximately 37km south-east of Dubbo on a 360-hectare site.\nIt holds a Long-Term Energy Service Agreement (LTESA) awarded through the fourth AEMO Services tender under the NSW Electricity Infrastructure Roadmap, making it one of the first solar-plus-storage projects to secure an LTESA under that framework.\nFrom groundbreaking to mechanical completion\nThe project has moved through construction in just under a year. As PV Tech reported when Gentari broke ground in August 2025, the site was positioned as one of Australia’s first large-scale DC-coupled solar and storage projects, with commercial operations targeted for early 2027.\nThe Maryvale project originated with solar developer Wirsol Energy Australia before Ge",
