     "title": "ZEBRE submits 180MW BESS to Australia’s EPBC Act as ZEN Energy administration fails to slow pipeline",
     "url": "https://www.energy-storage.news/zebre-submits-180mw-bess-to-australias-epbc-act-as-zen-energy-administration-fails-to-slow-pipeline/",
     "time": "08-07 11:53",
     "ts": 1786074800,
     "summary": "ZEBRE has submitted the 180MW/360MWh Noblevale Battery in Queensland for federal environmental assessment under the EPBC Act.",
     "source": "Energy Storage News",
     "content": "ZEBRE, a joint venture between Taiwanese renewable energy developer HD Renewable Energy (HDRE) and Australian developer ZEN Energy, has submitted the 180MW/360MWh Noblevale Battery in Queensland for federal environmental assessment under the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation (EPBC) Act.\nThe referral covers a standalone grid-scale battery energy storage system (BESS) to be located on a repurposed mining site near Swanbank, in the Ipswich City Council area, approximately 45km south-west of Brisbane.\nThe project has a power output of 180MW and a storage capacity of 360MWh, giving it a 2-hour duration at full power. ZEBRE notes the project capacity may increase while maintaining the same footprint, subject to battery technology improvements available at the time of construction\nIn doing so, the project aims to cater for the pace of improvements in lithium-ion cell energy density and system-level efficiency across successive generations of utility-scale BESS.\nTry Premium for just $1\n- Full premium access for the first month at only $1\n- Converts to an annual rate after 30 days unless cancelled\n- Cancel anytime during the trial period\nPremium Benefits\n- Expert industry analysis and interviews\n- Digital access to PV Tech Power journal\n- Exclusive event discounts\nOr get the full Premium subscription right away\nOr continue reading this article for free\nThe facility will connect to the National Electricity Market (NEM) via a new underground transmission cable running to the nearby Blackstone electrical substation.\nThe underground routing was selected to avoid impacts on native vegetation, habitat, and wildlife movement corridors, with the cable traversing an existing high-voltage overhead power line easement that is already devoid of native vegetation.\nThe BESS will be configured across three battery yards within a total disturbance footprint of 6.33 hectares. Infrastructure on site will include a substation, internal roads, a site office, a control building, a maintenance shed, underground wastewater and rainwater holding tanks, a stormwater detention basin, and koala-proof fencing.\nConstruction activities will include earthworks to level the site and create bench surfaces for the battery equipment; civil works to prepare equipment foundations and cable trenches; equipment delivery and installation; electrical cabling, connection, commissioning, and energisation.\nThe site is currently used for cattle grazing on degraded eucalypt woodland and exotic pasture.\nIts location within the Swanbank Industrial Area, a former coal and mining hub in south-east Queensland, means the project is sited close to existing transmission infrastructure, minimising the length of cable required for grid connection and reducing both costs and environmental disturbance compared with a greenfield transmission connection.\nThe Swanbank area, where Noblevale will be located, already hosts CleanCo Queensland’s 250MW/500MWh Swanbank BESS, which officially opened in F",
