     "title": "Here’s what the first mRNA flu vaccine could mean for flu season",
     "url": "https://www.sciencenews.org/article/first-mrna-flu-vaccine-fall-season",
     "time": "08-07 05:25",
     "ts": 1786051540,
     "summary": "Compared with standard flu shots, Moderna’s mFlusiva generates stronger, longer-lasting immunity and is more effective at preventing infection, data show.",
     "source": "Science News",
     "content": "Here’s what the first mRNA flu vaccine could mean for flu season\nModerna’s mFlusiva uses the same technology as COVID-19 vaccines\nmRNA flu shots have officially arrived.\nOn August 5, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approved the first influenza vaccine that uses the same technology underpinning COVID-19 shots. The flu vaccine, called mFlusiva and produced by Moderna, will be available for people ages 50 and older at select drug stores ahead of the upcoming flu season, the Massachusetts-based company said.\nInfluenza kills thousands of people in the United States each year and hospitalizes tens of thousands more. The FDA’s approval is based on a study of 40,000 individuals who got either a dose of mFlusiva or a conventional flu shot. The mRNA flu shot group experienced 27 percent fewer cases, clinical trial results showed.\nDepending on the year, conventional vaccines are anywhere from 40 percent to 60 percent effective at preventing infection. That’s often due to a mismatch between predictions for what flu strain will be circulating in the fall and which one actually shows up.\nOther research, published in June in Nature Immunology, found that the mRNA vaccine generates a stronger and longer-lasting immune response than conventional flu vaccines.\nScience News spoke with study coauthor Hanover Matz, an immunologist at WashU Medicine in St. Louis, about the new vaccine and its implications for future flu seasons. This conversation has been edited for clarity and length.\nSN: How is Moderna’s mRNA flu vaccine different from conventional flu vaccines?\nMatz: The conventional flu vaccine is typically generated in chicken eggs, and it’s what we call an inactivated flu vaccine. Essentially there are pieces of the virus that are used to teach your immune system how to fight flu, and because the virus is raised in chicken eggs, it takes a long time to generate that vaccine. This is an mRNA-based vaccine, which provides genetic information for how to make the proteins of the flu virus, and then that teaches your immune system how to fight it.\nSN: What are the benefits of getting the mRNA flu vaccine versus the conventional one?\nMatz: It can be produced much faster than the conventional flu vaccine, and this is very useful if the virus changes during the flu season.\nFor producing the egg-based vaccine, policy makers need to determine what the most likely strains of the flu virus are going to be for the fall season in the Northern Hemisphere. Typically, they need to start that production well before the fall to get the vaccine doses ready. If there’s a mismatch in strains, there’s really no way they can go back and update the vaccine and change it. The production just takes too long. With an mRNA-based vaccine, you could feasibly cut down that production time to a couple of months. [Manufacturers] have the potential to improve the speed with which they could update the vaccine if that strain mismatch became a problem during the during the flu season.\nIt also",
