     "title": "DeepMind AI gives an extra day of warning ahead of deadly cyclones",
     "url": "https://www.newscientist.com/article/2583547-deepmind-ai-gives-an-extra-day-of-warning-ahead-of-deadly-cyclones/?utm_campaign=RSS|NSNS&utm_content=home&utm_medium=RSS&utm_source=NSNS",
     "time": "08-06 23:00",
     "ts": 1786028400,
     "summary": "An AI model from DeepMind can predict cyclones three days ahead with a level of accuracy that previous models can only hit a day later",
     "source": "New Scientist",
     "content": "DeepMind has developed an AI model that can predict deadly cyclones one day further out than existing methods, potentially saving lives by allowing more accurate and timely decisions about evacuations.\nMany existing weather forecasts are based on physics simulations run on powerful supercomputers that model and extrapolate weather patterns as accurately as possible. AI models may represent a faster and less costly method.\nWeatherNext is able to generate a 15-day forecast in less than a minute on one of Google’s custom Tensor Processing Unit chips. Meanwhile, physics-based models can consume days of high-powered computer time.\nAdvertisement\nThe new model works on a simulation of the atmosphere where the smallest cell is 28 square kilometres, making it a hundred times less granular than traditional models. It was trained on nearly 20 terabytes of global atmospheric data, as well as data on 5000 historical storms, which Ferran Alet at DeepMind says was crucial to improving WeatherNext’s accuracy at predicting the behaviour of new cyclones.\nThe DeepMind team compared its WeatherNext AI model with Google’s GenCast model, the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts’s ENS model and the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration’s Hurricane Analysis and Forecast System. It found that WeatherNext was able to produce accurate predictions three days out that could only be matched by current models two days out, in terms of maximum wind speed and the distance error between a cyclone’s predicted location and actual eventual location.\n“The longer time goes [on], the worse you’re going to do, because weather is chaotic. So the question is, how much can we push this chaos barrier? How much can we see into the future?” says Alet. Because of the time taken to publish the work in an academic journal, he says that things have moved on since, and that DeepMind is actively working on building even faster and more accurate models.\nThe firm has a long history of attempting to improve weather forecasting. In 2021, it developed an extremely short-term and local model that could predict whether it would rain or not in the next 90 minutes more accurately than existing models. In 2023, it released a model that could provide accurate 10-day weather forecasts, and in 2024, it launched a tool that dramatically slashed the time and energy needed to make forecasts.\nHannah Cloke at the University of Reading, UK, who wasn’t involved in the research, says she has never experienced a faster and more dramatic shake-up in meteorology than the effect of AI in the past couple of years.\n“This is one of the most exciting fields to work in at the moment, and one of the reasons is the rise in machine-learning forecasting and the absolute speed and power with which we’re moving forward in this field,” she says. “You go on holiday for a week… and find out something else has happened.”\nCloke says that advances are far outpacing the speed of academic journals, meaning that the cu",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "DeepMind AI可将致命气旋预警提前一天",
     "content_zh": "DeepMind研发的AI模型可将致命气旋的预警时间比现有方法提前1天，助力更精准及时的疏散决策，挽救生命。现有天气预报多依赖超算运行的物理模拟，成本高耗时长，而新模型WeatherNext在谷歌定制张量处理单元芯片上1分钟内就能生成15天预报，效率远高于物理模型。该模型大气模拟最小单元格为28平方公里，精度是传统模型的百分之一，团队用近20TB全球大气数据及5000次历史风暴数据训练，大幅提升了新气旋行为预测的准确率，目前该模型已与谷歌GenCast模型、欧洲中期天气预报中心ENS模型完成对比。"
